<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
	<string>ContextForClaude</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>com.omi.context-for-claude</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Context for Claude</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Context for Claude</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>Context for Claude</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>1.0.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>1</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>14.4</string>
	<!-- Not menu-bar-only any more, and the direction of that change is the whole decision.

	     The app lives in two places: the status item, and the Dock. On a crowded menu bar a 16 pt
	     template mark is one of thirty near-identical glyphs, which is the report this answers.
	     `DockPresence` (Settings/SettingsPreferences.swift) owns the preference that hides the Dock
	     half again; this key decides which shape the process is *launched* in, which Swift cannot.

	     `false` rather than `true` because promotion is the transition that misbehaves. An app that
	     launches `LSUIElement` and calls `setActivationPolicy(.regular)` gets its Dock icon, but the
	     main menu AppKit builds around a regular app is not drawn until the app has been deactivated
	     and reactivated — so the default case would ship a menu bar that appears on the second
	     activation. Demotion is the direction that behaves, so the process launches regular and
	     `ContextAppDelegate` demotes it for the user who turned the row off. That user pays a Dock
	     icon visible for the length of a launch; nobody pays a missing menu bar.

	     Written out rather than deleted: absent and `false` mean the same thing to LaunchServices,
	     and a key that is present is a key the next person reads the reasoning for. -->
	<key>LSUIElement</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- macOS shows these verbatim in the TCC prompt, so they are written as the app speaking. -->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>I listen to your microphone so I can hear what you talk about and give Claude that context later.</string>
	<key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
	<string>I record system audio so I can catch the other side of your calls, not just your half.</string>
	<key>NSScreenCaptureUsageDescription</key>
	<string>I take an occasional snapshot of your screen so I can see what you are working on and tell Claude about it.</string>

	<!-- ==========================================================================================
	     Sparkle auto-update.

	     This app updates itself with Sparkle, the same mechanism `desktop/macos` uses, and shares
	     none of that app's identity: its own feed, its own EdDSA key pair, its own bundle. Sparkle
	     draws the update sheet from *this* bundle — `CFBundleName`, `CFBundleIconFile`,
	     `CFBundleShortVersionString` — and the release notes from the feed below, so a user is never
	     shown Omi's name, Omi's icon, or Omi's changelog. Nothing here may ever be pointed at
	     `api.omi.me/v2/desktop/appcast.xml`: that feed's enclosures are Omi Computer bundles, and
	     Sparkle would replace this app with one.

	     `SUPublicEDKey` ships as a placeholder on purpose. The private half lives only in the
	     releasing maintainer's login keychain and is never committed, so this repository cannot
	     contain a working signing key. `UpdatePolicy` treats the placeholder as "not configured" and
	     refuses to start Sparkle at all — load-bearing rather than tidy, because
	     `SPUStandardUpdaterController` calls `abort()` when it is started with an unusable key.
	     Generating the pair and filling this in is the first step of `docs/releasing.md`.
	     ========================================================================================== -->
	<key>SUFeedURL</key>
	<string>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/BasedHardware/omi/main/desktop/context-for-claude/appcast.xml</string>
	<key>SUPublicEDKey</key>
	<string>REPLACE_WITH_SUPublicEDKey_FROM_generate_keys</string>
	<key>SUEnableAutomaticChecks</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Deliberately false: an update is asked for, not applied behind the user's back. Replacing the
	     bundle is the one operation that can cost this app its Screen Recording grant, and a user who
	     watched themselves approve an update can connect a dead capture to a cause. A silent
	     overnight swap cannot be connected to anything. -->
	<key>SUAutomaticallyUpdate</key>
	<false/>
	<!-- Six hours. Omi checks every ten minutes because it publishes a candidate most days; this app
	     publishes rarely, and a background request every ten minutes for a feed that changes monthly
	     is chatter nobody asked for. -->
	<key>SUScheduledCheckInterval</key>
	<integer>21600</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
